program            = 006_short_circuit_evaluation
area               = 06_control_flow
description        = Short-circuit evaluation of && and ||, asserting that the right operand is NOT evaluated when the left operand settles the result, observed through a volatile counter
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. The property under test is an
absence, so the side-effect counter rhs_calls is declared volatile: a plain
counter could legally be removed by dead-code elimination, which would make the
assertion vacuous. Measured at -O2, the volatile form emits 74 accesses to the
counter against 34 for the plain form, confirming the qualifier is load-bearing.
Every object is assigned before it is read; no read of uninitialised storage
occurs. All arithmetic is on int values of small magnitude, so no signed
overflow is possible; the only division is 100 / a inside a guarded expression
whose left operand a != 0 is false when a is zero, and short-circuit evaluation
guarantees the division is never performed, so there is no division by zero.
There are no shifts, no pointers, no arrays and no type punning, therefore no
aliasing violation and no dependence on padding bytes. No object is modified
twice between sequence points. Each volatile object is read exactly once into a
plain local before being used in a logical expression, so no expression contains
two volatile accesses whose relative order would be unspecified. The && and ||
operators impose a sequence point between their operands, so the counter's value
at each print is fully determined. The single while loop is bounded by a < 3 and
its body increments a, so it terminates after exactly three iterations.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Nothing implementation-defined is observed. Only int values are printed, so the
measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned
on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot affect the output, and the measured width
difference for long and pointers (4 bytes on i686, 8 elsewhere) is never exposed
because no long, pointer, size_t or ptrdiff_t value is printed and no sizeof
result appears in the output. No address is printed. No floating point is used.
The results of &&, || and ! are normalised by the language to exactly 0 or 1, so
they are identical on every target. All four targets and all three optimization
levels are therefore compared, and all three oracles are enabled.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
const_and_false r=0 rhs_calls=0
const_or_true r=1 rhs_calls=0
const_and_true r=1 rhs_calls=1
const_or_false r=1 rhs_calls=1
runtime_and_false r=0 rhs_calls=0
runtime_or_true r=1 rhs_calls=0
runtime_and_true r=1 rhs_calls=1
runtime_or_false r=1 rhs_calls=1
chain_and_stop_at_first r=0 rhs_calls=0
chain_or_stop_at_first r=1 rhs_calls=0
chain_and_stop_midway r=0 rhs_calls=2
chain_or_stop_midway r=1 rhs_calls=2
mixed_grouping r=1 rhs_calls=1
guarded_divide_zero r=0 rhs_calls=0
guarded_divide_one r=1
if_and_short_circuit r=2 rhs_calls=0
if_or_short_circuit r=1 rhs_calls=0
while_short_circuit a=3 rhs_calls=3
normalisation not=0 notnot=1 and=1 or=1
normalisation_zero not=1 notnot=0 and=0 or=0
END
